1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,310
This is an AI scientist for GPU rental prices.

2
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:14,063
It forecasts thirty-seven posted GPU prices one, three and seven days ahead, and tests which kinds of AI help make those forecasts better on data the system has not seen.

3
00:00:15,063 --> 00:00:18,145
Each published price is the middle of a list of offers.

4
00:00:18,145 --> 00:00:19,602
So we asked two questions.

5
00:00:19,602 --> 00:00:23,189
Does looking at the whole list beat looking at the median alone?

6
00:00:23,189 --> 00:00:25,823
And which kinds of AI help survive a fair test?

7
00:00:26,823 --> 00:00:27,597
The headline.

8
00:00:27,597 --> 00:00:34,561
The forecast we hand in made twenty percent less error than drift, the reference forecast, on days no model had seen.

9
00:00:34,561 --> 00:00:39,978
That is every forecaster, scored on the same four hundred and seventy-nine fresh forecasts.

10
00:00:40,978 --> 00:00:41,842
The key insight.

11
00:00:41,842 --> 00:00:49,245
Two thirds of the movement in a published median comes from offers joining or leaving the list, not from providers changing their prices.

12
00:00:50,245 --> 00:00:56,494
So our engine replays what each offer did on past days, applies that to today's list, and takes the middle again.

13
00:00:56,494 --> 00:00:59,756
The spread of those replayed middles is the forecast range.

14
00:01:00,756 --> 00:01:02,322
Here it is step by step.

15
00:01:02,322 --> 00:01:04,476
Offers reprice, leave, or rejoin.

16
00:01:04,476 --> 00:01:08,914
Each replay gives a new median, and together they form the forecast.

17
00:01:09,914 --> 00:01:19,357
On top of the engine sit five AI agents, each with one job: a list reader, a repricer watcher, a base-rate keeper, a reference weigher and a jump spotter.

18
00:01:19,357 --> 00:01:21,135
Their forecasts are averaged.

19
00:01:22,135 --> 00:01:24,951
Training is a recursive self-improvement loop.

20
00:01:24,951 --> 00:01:29,298
Each round, the crowd forecasts practice days and a verifier scores it.

21
00:01:29,298 --> 00:01:31,196
A coach AI proposes one change.

22
00:01:31,196 --> 00:01:37,073
A second verifier keeps it only if practice days improve and hidden check days do not get worse.

23
00:01:37,073 --> 00:01:40,134
The verifiers are fixed code the AI cannot change.

24
00:01:40,134 --> 00:01:45,460
Ten rounds, then a final exam on unseen days: four percent better than the first crowd.

25
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,538
At inference the crowd is frozen.

26
00:01:48,538 --> 00:01:55,148
For each price, the five agents read the facts, their answers are averaged, and the ranges are stretched.

27
00:01:55,148 --> 00:01:58,484
One pass per price, one hundred and eleven forecasts.

28
00:01:59,484 --> 00:02:10,479
The test was written down before the results existed: a committed protocol, a time machine that only sees past data, shuffled worlds as a placebo, and exam days no model had seen.

29
00:02:11,479 --> 00:02:18,918
Across three separate tests, the engine beat drift by about eight percent, and the five agents by fifteen to nineteen percent.

30
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:21,277
Not every idea worked.

31
00:02:21,277 --> 00:02:29,862
Of seven ways to make the system improve itself, two held up: the coach, and reinforcement learning that teaches a small model to forecast.

32
00:02:29,862 --> 00:02:32,579
Three did not, and two were not established.

33
00:02:33,579 --> 00:02:34,647
One honest caveat.

34
00:02:34,647 --> 00:02:42,184
Ranges held the truth eighty percent of the time overall, but only sixty-six percent of the time when the price actually moved.

35
00:02:43,184 --> 00:02:44,709
Three things to remember.

36
00:02:44,709 --> 00:02:45,989
Look inside the list.

37
00:02:45,989 --> 00:02:50,319
Five AI agents plus a coach cut error by twenty percent on unseen days.

38
00:02:50,319 --> 00:02:53,308
And most self-improvement ideas fail a fair test.

39
00:02:53,308 --> 00:02:56,235
The verifier is what makes the loop trustworthy.
